### Dependence on B_{0}

The property of the saturated MRI-driven turbulence is examined by varying B_{0} from 10^{11} to 5\\times 10^{13} G without changing the initial equilibrium model.

Figure 5 (a) shows the B_{0}-dependence of the mean magnetic energy density \\langle\\langle\\epsilon_{M}\\rangle\\rangle, where the single angular brackets denote the volume average spanning in the range of r_{\\rm in}\\leq r\\leq r_{\\rm out} and the additional angular brackets denote the time average in the range of t_{s}\\leq t\\leq t_{e} [r_{\\rm in} (r_{\\rm out}) is 10 km (80 km) and t_{s} (t_{e}) is 30 ms (40 ms) for all the models]. The red circle represents the MRI-turbulent model and the green cross is the model in which the MRI does not grow. The orange dotted line is the reference slope with \\propto B_{0}^{2}. The expected magnetic energy stored in the full spherical shell of r_{\\rm in}\\leq r\\leq r_{\\rm out}, which is defined by

E_{\\rm mag}=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi(r_{\\rm out}^{3}-r_{\\rm in}^{3})\\langle\\langle \\epsilon_{M}\\rangle\\rangle\\;,  is presented in the right axis just for the reference.

In the range B_{0}\\ll 10^{13} G, \\langle\\langle\\epsilon_{M}\\rangle\\rangle is proportional to B_{0}^{2}. This is similar to the recent studies of the MRI in the stratified accretion disk (e.g., Suzuki et al. 2010). The dependency becomes weaker with the increase of B_{0} and \\langle\\langle\\epsilon_{M}\\rangle\\rangle hits the ceiling at \\epsilon_{\\rm upper}\\simeq 4\\times 10^{27} erg/cm{}^{3} in the range B_{0}\\gtrsim 10^{13} G. Except for the most strongly magnetized model in which the MRI does not grow, whole the convectively stable region around the neutrinosphere is overwhelmed with the MHD turbulence.

